<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alan Lomax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alan Lomax was an American ethnomusicologist and folklorist, known for his field recordings of folk music. The son of the folklorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lomax">John Lomax</a>, he joined his father's collecting trips for the Library of Congress as a teenager and from 1937 to 1942 was Assistant in Charge of its Archive of Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, recording <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a>, Lead Belly and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> Delta, Muddy Waters and Son House. After Congress ended that funding in 1942 he collected independently in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. His recordings, radio shows and concerts helped start the American and British folk revivals, and he later developed Cantometrics and argued for what he called cultural equity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alan-lomax.jpg" alt="Alan Lomax" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alan_Lomax.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
